which flourished in the seas of the Carboniferous and Permian periods, and which have been referred to the families ''Pleuracanthidæ'' and ''Cladodontidæ''.

'''Xenaltica''' (zē-nal′ti-kä), ''n.'' [NL. (Baly, 1875), < Gr. ξένος, strange, + NL. ''Haltica'', q. v.] A genus of beetles, of the family ''Chrysomelidæ'', having the four anterior tibiæ with a small spine and the hind tibiæ with a double spine. {{smaller|The two known species are from Old Calabar and Madagascar. The genus is supposed to be synonymous with ''Myrcina'' (Chapuis, 1875).}}

'''xenarthral''' (zē-när′thral), ''a.'' [< Gr. ξένος, strange, + ἄρθρον, a joint.] Peculiarly or strangely jointed, as a mammal’s vertebræ;
[[FIGURE]]
{{c|{{smaller|Xenarthral Articulation of Twelfth and Thirteenth Dorsal Vertebræ of Great Ant-eater (''Myrmecophaga jubata''), side view, two thirds natural size.}}}}
{{c|{{smaller|''az'', prezygapophysis, with ''az′'', additional anterior articular facet; ''pz'', postzygapophysis, with ''pz''<sup>1</sup>, ''pz''<sup>2</sup>, additional posterior articular facets; ''m'', metapophysis; ''cc'', facet for articulation of capitellum of rib; ''tc'', the same for tubercle of rib.}}}}
having certain accessory articulations of the dorsolumbar vertebræ, as the American edentates: the opposite of ''nomarthral''. ''Gill'', 1884.

'''xenelasia''' (zen-ē-lā′si-ä), ''n.'' [NL., < Gr. ξενηλασία, the expulsion of strangers, an alien act, < ξένος, a stranger, + ἔλασις, < ἐλαύνειν (ἐλα-), drive.] A Spartan law or alien act which prohibited strangers from residing in Sparta without permission.
